{#
  _panel_grant.j2 — the SINGLE renderer for a panel's roster grant.

  A panel whose service is reached by each per-user terminal with the operator
  secret ITS container holds needs that service's compose to hand it the
  entitled users' secrets and say whose each one is. Every compose template
  serving a panel — the bundled `app_runtime` and `bluesky_web` templates and
  an application's own `image:` fragment — imports this macro rather than
  writing the block itself, so the services cannot drift into dialects of
  "accept these operators".

  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  IMPORTING
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

  The macro takes everything it needs as a parameter, so a plain (context-free)
  import is correct — never `with context`:

      {% import "services/_panel_grant.j2" as panel %}

  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE `credentials` ARGUMENT
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

  `panel_grant.credentials` of the render context (`PanelGrant` in
  deployment/web_terminals/roster.py), one mapping per credential the panel's
  gate accepts, roster order:

    var       the `OSPREY_TERMINAL_SECRET_<SUFFIX>` variable handed to the
              container;
    source    the deploy variable its value is interpolated from;
    owner     the account an admitted request carrying it is recorded as;
    required  whether `source` is read with no default.

  A roster user's credential reads its own variable with an empty default: a
  user with no secret in the deploy .env is simply not admitted. The single-user
  alias of a roster-less deployment reads `OSPREY_TERMINAL_SECRET` with none:
  empty, it would lock out the one account that deployment has. Every user's
  terminal proxies the panel here with the secret their own container holds
  (under the fixed `OSPREY_TERMINAL_SECRET` name, interpolated from their own
  variable), so no user's container is ever handed the deployment-wide secret,
  and a user purged from the roster is refused here from the next
  `osprey build` on (the start verbs are as-built and re-render nothing). Only
  the entitled users are listed; the others get no key to this service. The
  accept flag is what makes web_auth ACCEPT the variables it finds: every
  process that loads the deploy .env sees them all, and only this service may
  treat them as its operators.

  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE OWNERS MAP
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

  `OSPREY_TERMINAL_ROSTER_OWNERS` is comma-separated `<SUFFIX>=<owner>` pairs
  in the grant's own order. The gate admits any of the granted secrets and
  names the operator behind a request by the one that matched; without the map
  a match says only which slot the credential filled, not whose account it is,
  and every panel action the deployment records would name nobody. A variable
  alone cannot answer that: it survives compose interpolation as a value, not
  as a name. Not a secret and no interpolation: the owners are already in the
  rendered roster.

  Each key is sliced off the variable this macro itself emits rather than
  derived from the owner a second time, so a key cannot name a variable the
  service was not handed. The literal stripped is `TERMINAL_SECRET_VAR_PREFIX`
  (deployment/web_terminals/auth_credentials.py), pinned to it and to web_auth's
  parse by tests/deployment/test_panel_grant_macro.py. The render refuses a
  roster name that is not a single `[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*` component,
  which is what makes `,` and `=` safe as the map's punctuation.

  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHITESPACE CONTRACT
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

  As in `_env_axis.j2`: the macro emits a LEADING newline and no trailing one,
  and is called alone on its own line with a left-trim marker inside an
  `environment:` block:

      {{- panel.env(panel_grant.credentials) }}

  With no credentials it emits the empty string and the line disappears.

  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHAT AN EDIT HERE REDDENS
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Every template that imports this file renders its macro, and those renders
  are pinned byte for byte. Regenerate in the same change. One command per
  suite that pins an importer of this file:

      PYTHONPATH=src ./.venv/bin/python tests/templates/test_render_defaults_golden.py
      PYTHONPATH=src ./.venv/bin/python tests/templates/test_render_axis_shapes.py
#}

{%- macro env(credentials) -%}
{%- if credentials %}
      # THE ROSTER GRANT. Every web-terminal user whose login shows this
      # panel proxies it here with the operator secret their own container
      # holds. Listing each of those variables lets this container's web gate
      # accept any of them, so no user's container is handed another's secret;
      # the flag is what makes the gate accept the listed variables.
      OSPREY_TERMINAL_ACCEPT_ROSTER_SECRETS: "1"
      # A roster user's variable reads itself, empty by default; an alias reads
      # the deploy variable that holds its secret, with no default: empty, it
      # would lock out the one account a roster-less deployment has.
{%- for c in credentials %}
      {{ c.var }}: ${{ "{" }}{{ c.source }}{{ "" if c.required else ":-" }}{{ "}" }}
{%- endfor %}
      # WHOSE each of those secrets is, as `<SUFFIX>=<username>` pairs.
      OSPREY_TERMINAL_ROSTER_OWNERS: "{% for c in credentials %}{{ c.var | replace("OSPREY_TERMINAL_SECRET_", "", 1) }}={{ c.owner }}{{ "," if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}"
{%- endif %}
{%- endmacro -%}
